<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I&#8217;ve been running Apple&#8217;s beta software this year and I keep forgetting it&#8217;s there. And that got me thinking.</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This is the smoothest beta cycle in years. Apple shipped one genuinely big new thing, Siri AI, the one we&#8217;ve all been waiting on, and then spent the rest of the summer fixing bugs and sanding down rough edges instead of stacking six more headline features on top. They delivered on a prior promise instead of making three shiny new ones they couldn&#8217;t keep.</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you are listening, Apple, I think this is the way things should work going forward. Each year, solve one big problem and polish everything else.</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Mac, the iPhone, and the iPad are grown up. iOS is nearing twenty years old. These platforms don&#8217;t need seven or eight tentpole features a year to justify themselves anymore. Nobody is buying an iPhone because it added a dozen things you&#8217;ll never open. Pick one big thing. Do it really well. Spend the other eleven months making the thousand small things people actually touch every day feel solid. Make that the plan every year, not the emergency measure you reach for after a rough patch.</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I&#8217;ve even got a list! Here are three nominations for next year.</p>

<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Global, Reliable Device Search</h2>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The first is search that actually understands me. Most people have no idea how to find anything on their own device, and it&#8217;s not their fault. Spotlight still treats my files, my Mail, my Messages, and my Photos like separate keyword boxes that don&#8217;t talk to each other. I know the photo exists. I know I mentioned the thing in a text and then again in an email, but I have to go rummage through four apps to reassemble one memory. A local, secure, AI-powered search that actually understood what I meant and searched everywhere at once would be a massive jump for the average person. It would help people like me too, the ones who already know every keyboard shortcut. Microsoft has already gone down this road with Windows. I think Apple could do the same thing more safely and with a bit more panache. This is worth a full year of focus on its own.</p>

<h2 class="wp-block-heading">A Quality AI Harness</h2>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The second is a real on-device AI harness. I&#8217;ve said before that I haven&#8217;t found a good harness for local AI models yet, and I mean that plainly. The frontier models have moved fast. Claude has Cowork, this whole scaffolding around the model that lets it actually do multi-step work for me. The small models running right on your Mac or your iPhone have nothing like that. They answer a question and stop. If Apple built the harness so on-device Apple Intelligence could take an instruction and carry it through several steps on its own, that would be the natural next chapter after shipping Siri AI.</p>

<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Make Apple Home Awesome</h2>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The third is Apple Home. I&#8217;ve got a house full of HomeKit gear, and I still can&#8217;t fully trust it. Scenes drop. An automation that ran fine for a month stops firing, and I don&#8217;t find out until something doesn&#8217;t happen. A light that should turn on at sunset decides tonight is the night it won&#8217;t. I don&#8217;t want new Home features. I want a year where the whole team does nothing but make the thing I already own boringly, gloriously reliable.</p>

<p class="wp-block-paragraph">I&#8217;m not predicting any of this will happen. I just wish it would. One big, useful update a year, and a long list of bug fixes like we actually got this time. I want the stuff I own to work. I want the promises kept. Give me one big new thing done right, and then leave the rest of the year to make my Mac feel like an old friend who has their act together.</p>

